    mg_ch('2. CRUD & Operators Mastery', 'Querying Arrays & Embedded Documents: The Critical $elemMatch Rule', 'mongodb array queries, $elemMatch vs dot notation, $all $size mongodb',
        'Querying arrays of primitives or arrays of embedded objects has a crucial rule that trips up even experienced developers: **dot notation evaluates conditions across the entire array independently**, whereas **`$elemMatch` requires a SINGLE array element to satisfy ALL conditions simultaneously**!',
        ['**Why `{ scores: { $gt: 80, $lt: 85 } }` Can Match `[70, 95]`!**: On an array field `scores: [70, 95]`, `95` satisfies `$gt: 80` and `70` satisfies `$lt: 85`, so the document matches even though **no single score** is between 80 and 85! Writing `{ scores: { $elemMatch: { $gt: 80, $lt: 85 } } }` enforces that **one** element satisfies both!', '**Exact Object Match Trap**: `{ tags: { k: "env", v: "prod" } }` checks **exact BSON key order** (`{ v: "prod", k: "env" }` fails!); use dot notation or `$elemMatch` instead!'],
        [['`$all: ["MongoDB", "Docker"]`', 'Array contains ALL listed elements regardless of order', 'Works with Multikey indexes.'], ['`$size: 3`', 'Array has exact length 3', '`$size` cannot take ranges (`$gt`)—maintain a `skillsCount` field if querying array length ranges!'], ['`$elemMatch: { qty: { $gt: 5 }, price: { $lt: 20 } }`', 'At least ONE array element matches ALL inner predicates', 'Mandatory whenever filtering an array on 2+ conditions!']],
        "db.users.find({ skills: { \$all: ['MongoDB'] } })",
        ['**Golden Array Rule**: Anytime you have **two or more** conditions on an array field (or on subfields of an array of objects like `items.sku` AND `items.qty`), **always use `$elemMatch`**!'],
        [['question' => 'Given a document with scores: [60, 95], does db.exams.find({ scores: { $gt: 80, $lt: 70 } }) match it?', 'answer' => 'Yes! Because 95 > 80 and 60 < 70 across the array—use $elemMatch to require one element to match both!', 'options' => ['Yes! Because 95 > 80 and 60 < 70 across the array—use $elemMatch to require one element to match both!', 'No, MongoDB throws an impossible range error', 'No, only the first element is checked', 'Only if scores is sorted']], ['question' => 'Why can\'t you write { tags: { $size: { $gt: 5 } } } in MongoDB?', 'answer' => '$size accepts only an exact integer literal; store a counter field or use $expr: { $gt: [{ $size: "$tags" }, 5] }', 'options' => ['$size accepts only an exact integer literal; store a counter field or use $expr: { $gt: [{ $size: "$tags" }, 5] }', 'Arrays have max size 4', '$gt only works on strings', 'MongoDB forbids counting arrays']], ['question' => 'Why is matching a whole subdocument { addr: { city: "Pune", pin: "411001" } } brittle?', 'answer' => 'Whole-subdocument equality requires exact field order and zero extra fields in BSON', 'options' => ['Whole-subdocument equality requires exact field order and zero extra fields in BSON', 'Subdocuments cannot be indexed', 'Numbers are converted to strings', 'It deletes unmatched keys']]],
        'Run `db.users.find({ skills: { $all: [\'MongoDB\'] } })` in the playground.'
    ),
    mg_ch('2. CRUD & Operators Mastery', 'Updating Documents: Atomic Operators ($set, $inc, $push, $addToSet, $pull) & Upserts', 'mongodb updateOne updateMany, $set $inc $addToSet $pull, findOneAndUpdate returnDocument, arrayFilters',
        'Atomic update operators mutate fields in-place inside WiredTiger without read-modify-write race conditions—supporting counters (`$inc`), set-semantics (`$addToSet`), conditional array updates (`arrayFilters`), **`upsert: true`**, and **`findOneAndUpdate(..., { returnDocument: "after" })`**.',
        ['**Never Omit `$set` in `updateOne()`**: Passing a plain object without `$`-operators (`replaceOne` style) is rejected by `updateOne()` to protect you from accidentally wiping all other fields!', '**`$push` vs `$addToSet`**: `$push` appends duplicates freely; `$addToSet` appends **only if the value is not already present**.', '**`$setOnInsert` with `upsert: true`**: Sets fields like `createdAt` *only* when an upsert inserts a brand-new document, leaving `createdAt` untouched on updates!'],
        [['`$inc: { stock: -1, sold: 1 }`', 'Atomic server-side increment/decrement', 'Combine with filter `{ sku: "X", stock: { $gte: 1 } }` to prevent negative stock!'], ['`$addToSet: { skills: "Rust" }`', 'Idempotent unique array append', 'Combine with `$each: [...]` to add multiple unique items at once.'], ['`findOneAndUpdate(q, u, { returnDocument: "after" })`', 'Atomically mutates and returns updated doc', 'Defaults to `"before"` (old doc) unless `"after"` is specified!']],
        "db.products.updateOne({ sku: 'LAP-PRO-16', stock: { \$gte: 1 } }, { \$inc: { stock: -1 } })",
        ['**Atomic Inventory Pattern**: `db.products.updateOne({ sku: "LAP-PRO-16", stock: { $gte: qty } }, { $inc: { stock: -qty } })` prevents overselling stock across 1,000 concurrent buyers in a **single atomic operation** without needing multi-document transactions!'],
        [['question' => 'In db.users.findOneAndUpdate(filter, update), which version of the document is returned by default if returnDocument is omitted?', 'answer' => 'The original document BEFORE the update was applied ("before")', 'options' => ['The original document BEFORE the update was applied ("before")', 'The modified document AFTER the update ("after")', 'Only a boolean true', 'An ObjectId string']], ['question' => 'Which update operator sets a field ONLY if an upsert operation inserts a new document (ignoring it on existing docs)?', 'answer' => '$setOnInsert', 'options' => ['$setOnInsert', '$setOnce', '$initField', '$default']], ['question' => 'What is the difference between $push and $addToSet on an array field?', 'answer' => '$addToSet only appends the element if it does not already exist in the array', 'options' => ['$addToSet only appends the element if it does not already exist in the array', '$push sorts the array alphabetically', '$addToSet deletes existing items', '$push works only on integers']]],
        'Execute the atomic stock decrement `updateOne` in the playground and inspect `matchedCount` and `modifiedCount`.'
    ),
    mg_ch('3. Schema Modeling, Validation & Indexing', 'NoSQL Schema Design Patterns & Anti-Patterns (Embedding vs Referencing)', 'mongodb schema design patterns, embedding vs referencing, bucket pattern, outlier pattern, subset pattern',
        'In relational SQL you model data around normalization (3NF); in **MongoDB you model schemas around your application\'s read/write access patterns**—choosing cleanly between Embedding, Referencing, Subset, Bucket, and Computed patterns.',
        ['**When to Embed**: `1:1` or bounded `1:Few` (`< 100` sub-items like user shipping addresses or order line-items) that are read together with the parent.', '**When to Reference (`ObjectId` array or child parent-ref)**: Unbounded `1:Many` / `1:Squillions` (e.g., 2M log events per host), `M:N` relationships where both sides mutate independently, or sub-entities exceeding 16MB.', '**Bucket Pattern (IoT / Time-Series)**: Group 60 per-minute temperature readings into 1 hourly document (`count: 60, sum: ..., readings: [...]`), shrinking index size by **60x**!'],
        [['`Subset Pattern`', 'Embed top 10 latest reviews on `product`; store remaining 5,000 in `reviews` collection', 'Product page loads in 1 fast read without bloating working-set RAM!'], ['`Bucket Pattern`', 'Group time-series events into fixed-window documents', 'Reduces index entries and disk seeks by 10x–100x.'], ['`Extended Reference Pattern`', 'Copy frequently displayed immutable fields (`customerName`) onto `order`', 'Avoids `$lookup` joins on order history listings!']],
        "db.orders.find().limit(3)",
        ['**#1 MongoDB Anti-Pattern**: Unbounded arrays! Every time a growing array reallocates on disk and updates Multikey indexes, write amplification spikes—use parent references (`{ productId: ObjectId(...) }` on `reviews`) when cardinality is unbounded.'],
        [['question' => 'Which MongoDB schema pattern embeds only the 10 most recent reviews inside a Product document while storing older reviews in a separate collection?', 'answer' => 'The Subset Pattern', 'options' => ['The Subset Pattern', 'The Sharded Join Pattern', 'Third Normal Form', 'The Polymorphic Anti-Pattern']], ['question' => 'Why is embedding an unbounded list of 500,000 comments inside a single Post document an anti-pattern?', 'answer' => 'It hits the 16MB BSON limit and causes severe write/RAM amplification', 'options' => ['It hits the 16MB BSON limit and causes severe write/RAM amplification', 'Arrays in MongoDB hold at most 10 items', 'BSON cannot nest objects', 'find() cannot read arrays']], ['question' => 'Which pattern groups IoT sensor readings into 1-hour documents containing up to 60 measurements?', 'answer' => 'The Bucket Pattern', 'options' => ['The Bucket Pattern', 'The EAV Pattern', 'The Star Schema', 'The Singleton Pattern']]],
        'Design an `orders` document using the Extended Reference pattern storing snapshot `{ userId, customerName, items: [...] }`.'
    ),
    mg_ch('3. Schema Modeling, Validation & Indexing', 'Schema Validation with $jsonSchema & TTL Auto-Expiring Collections', 'mongodb jsonSchema validation, validationLevel validationAction, mongodb ttl index expireAfterSeconds',
        '"Schema-flexible" does NOT mean unvalidated chaos: MongoDB enforces server-side **`$jsonSchema` validation** right inside `mongod` and automatically purges expired sessions/OTPs via **TTL (Time-To-Live) Indexes**.',
        ['**`$jsonSchema` Rules**: Enforce `required` fields, `bsonType` (`"string"`, `"int"`, `"decimal"`, `"objectId"`), numeric `minimum`/`maximum`, `enum` sets, and `additionalProperties: false`.', '**Gradual Rollout on Legacy Collections (`validationLevel: "moderate"`)**: `"strict"` validates all inserts/updates; `"moderate"` validates new inserts and already-conforming docs while letting legacy non-conforming docs be updated safely during migrations!', '**TTL Background Thread**: `db.sessions.createIndex({ expiresAt: 1 }, { expireAfterSeconds: 0 })` deletes documents automatically every ~60 seconds once `expiresAt` passes!'],
        [['`validationAction: "error"` vs `"warn"`', 'Rejects invalid writes vs logs warning in `mongod`', 'Start with `"warn"` in production for 24h before flipping to `"error"`!'], ['`expireAfterSeconds: 3600` on `ISODate` field', 'Auto-deletes docs 1 hour after timestamp', '**Gotcha**: If the field holds a string `"2026-01-01"` instead of `ISODate`, TTL NEVER deletes it!'], ['`expireAfterSeconds: 0`', 'Deletes document at the exact per-document `expiresAt` `ISODate`', 'Allows each row (e.g., 5m OTP vs 30d session) to have a custom TTL!']],
        "db.users.createIndex({ email: 1 }, { unique: true })",
        ['**Classic TTL Bug**: TTL indexes ONLY work on single-field indexes containing a **`Date` (`ISODate`)** (or array of `Date`s)—they CANNOT be compound indexes, and storing Unix epoch integers or ISO strings silently prevents deletion!'],
        [['question' => 'Why might a MongoDB TTL index silently fail to delete expired session documents?', 'answer' => 'The indexed field was stored as an ISO string or integer epoch instead of a BSON Date (ISODate)', 'options' => ['The indexed field was stored as an ISO string or integer epoch instead of a BSON Date (ISODate)', 'TTL requires multi-document transactions', 'WiredTiger does not support TTL', 'TTL only runs on February 29']], ['question' => 'How do you give different documents in the same collection different expiration lifetimes using a single TTL index?', 'answer' => 'Store a future expiresAt ISODate per document and set expireAfterSeconds: 0', 'options' => ['Store a future expiresAt ISODate per document and set expireAfterSeconds: 0', 'Create 100 separate TTL indexes', 'Use a compound TTL index', 'Set expireAfterSeconds: -1']], ['question' => 'Which $jsonSchema setting logs schema violations without rejecting writes while auditing production traffic?', 'answer' => 'validationAction: "warn"', 'options' => ['validationAction: "warn"', 'validationLevel: "off"', 'strict: false', 'dryRun: true']]],
        'Run `db.users.createIndex({ email: 1 }, { unique: true })` in the playground.'
